90,5187,2555,92,481,17,24984,481,22,14,17,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,220,17,18481,317,1502,31732,39,8,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,481,16,14,18,24984,220,22,14,16,17,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,220,18,18481,317,24157,6405,2533,6269,279,11931,315,279,4396,2606,11,18663,553,76602,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA line has slope $-\\frac{7}{4}.$  Which of the following vectors are possible direction vectors for the line?\n\n[asy]\nusepackage(\"amsmath\");\n\nunitsize(1 cm);\n\npair x = (3,0), y = (0,2);\n\nlabel(\"(A) $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}$\", y);\nlabel(\"(B) $\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$\", x + y);\nlabel(\"(C) $\\begin{pmatrix} -4/7 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$\", 2*x + y);\nlabel(\"(D) $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 7/4 \\end{pmatrix}$\", 3*x + y);\nlabel(\"(E) $\\begin{pmatrix} 14 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}$\", (0,0));\nlabel(\"(F) $\\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix}$\", x);\nlabel(\"(G) $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -7/2 \\end{pmatrix}$\", 2*x);\nlabel(\"(H) $\\begin{pmatrix} -1/3 \\\\ 7/12 \\end{pmatrix}$\", 3*x);\n[/asy]\n\nEnter the letters of the correct options, separated by commas.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"20f5d0c1a204343230e3823f9ac963fcde14d1fdffca5dbb0fd56e2fb2aaca09"},"seconds":17.830512523651123,"seed":433902800338433538,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine which of the given vectors are possible direction vectors for a line with slope \\(-\\frac{7}{4}\\), we need to recall that the slope of a line can also be represented by the ratio of the y-component to the x-component of its direction vector. Specifically, if a vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}\\) is a direction vector for the line, then the slope \\(m\\) of the line is given by \\(\\frac{b}{a}\\).\n\nGiven that the slope of the line is \\(-\\frac{7}{4}\\), we need to find vectors \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}\\) such that \\(\\frac{b}{a} = -\\frac{7}{4}\\). This implies that \\(b = -\\frac{7}{4}a\\). We will check each option to see if it satisfies this condition.\n\n(A) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{4} \\neq -\\frac{7}{4}\n\\]\nThis vector is not a direction vector.\n\n(B) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{7} \\neq -\\frac{7}{4}\n\\]\nThis vector is not a direction vector.\n\n(C) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -4/7 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{-4/7} = -\\frac{7}{4}\n\\]\nThis vector is a direction vector.\n\n(D) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 7/4 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\\[\n\\frac{7/4}{1} = \\frac{7}{4} \\neq -\\frac{7}{4}\n\\]\nThis vector is not a direction vector.\n\n(E) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 14 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\\[\n\\frac{-8}{14} = -\\frac{4}{7} \\neq -\\frac{7}{4}\n\\]\nThis vector is not a direction vector.\n\n(F) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{-12} = -\\frac{7}{4}\n\\]\nThis vector is a direction vector.\n\n(G) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -7/2 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\\[\n\\frac{-7/2}{-2} = \\frac{7}{4} \\neq -\\frac{7}{4}\n\\]\nThis vector is not a direction vector.\n\n(H) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -1/3 \\\\ 7/12 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\\[\n\\frac{7/12}{-1/3} = \\frac{7}{12} \\cdot \\frac{-3}{1} = -\\frac{7}{4}\n\\]\nThis vector is a direction vector.\n\nThus, the possible direction vectors are (C), (F), and (H). The answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{C,F,H}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{C,F,H}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:98"}
